      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pasadena was founded in 1893 by Galveston businessman John H. Burnett, who named it after Pasadena, California because of its lush vegetation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasadena%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "When the Galveston, Houston & Henderson Railroad crossed the Allen ranch in 1894, farmers settled the area and shipped produce by train.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasadena%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Pasadena Cemetery, established in 1906 and later called Crown Hill, contains graves of Civil War, Spanish‑American, WWI, and WWII veterans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://historicalcommission.harriscountytx.gov/Local-History/Topics-of-General-Interest/pasadena",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1980 film Urban Cowboy, shot in Pasadena, highlighted the city’s honky‑tonk bar Gilley’s, co‑owned by country star Mickey Gilley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasadena%2C_Texas",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pasadena celebrates its agricultural heritage each year with the Pasadena Strawberry Festival, drawing locals and visitors alike.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
